\title{Data-Valued Dependent-Product Fixed Points:\\Non-Collapse, a Boolean Model, and Finite Cardinality}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{31 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study self-indexed dependent-product equations of the form
\[
T\simeqv\prod_{x\in T}F(x),
\]
where $T$ is a type and $F(x)$ is a type of data attached to $x$. The behavior differs sharply from the proposition-valued variant. When every fiber is a proposition, proof irrelevance makes the section space a subsingleton and forces $T$ to be a singleton. For data-valued fibers this collapse fails. We give an explicit genuinely dependent solution over the Boolean type: the fiber over $\false$ is Boolean, while the fiber over $\true$ is a singleton. Evaluation at $\false$ and insertion of the unique value at $\true$ give inverse maps between Booleans and sections. The fibers are inequivalent and the section space has two elements. For arbitrary finite $T$ and finite fibers, we prove
\[
|T|=\prod_{x\in T}|F(x)|.
\]
We interpret this identity as a distribution-of-information law, describe algorithms for finite examples, contrast dependent and constant families, and formulate classification problems suggested by the Boolean model.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Fixed-point equations indexed by their own solution space occur naturally in logic, semantics, combinatorics, and data structures. A transparent form asks for a type $T$ and a family $F:T\to\Type$ such that $T$ is equivalent to the type of all sections of $F$:
\[
T\simeqv\prod_{x\in T}F(x).
\]
A section chooses, for each $x\in T$, a value in the possibly varying fiber $F(x)$. The equation says that one global state can be reversibly represented by one choice in every local fiber.

Self-indexing alone does not imply paradox, incompleteness, or undecidability. The character of the equation depends on the codomain of the family. If the fibers are propositions, their inhabitants carry no distinguishable choice: pointwise proof irrelevance collapses the entire product. If the fibers are ordinary data types, local alternatives survive and may encode nontrivial global states.

We develop the first nontrivial data-valued example and its finite arithmetic invariant. Put a two-element fiber over one Boolean point and a singleton fiber over the other. A section stores exactly one bit. Reading that bit decodes the section; writing it and filling the remaining coordinate with its unique value encodes a Boolean. Unequal fiber cardinalities make the construction genuinely dependent.

Our main results are proposition-valued collapse, an explicit Boolean data fixed point, inequivalence of its fibers, non-collapse of its section space, and the finite cardinality product law. The distinction between propositions and data is the conceptual center: the Boolean model demonstrates existence, while the product law begins the classification theory.

\section{Definitions and the proposition-valued comparison}

\begin{definition}[Dependent family and section]
Let $T$ be a type. A dependent family over $T$ assigns a type $F(x)$ to every $x\in T$. A section is a dependent function $s$ with $s(x)\in F(x)$ for every $x$. The section type is denoted $\prod_{x\in T}F(x)$.
\end{definition}

When $F(x)=A$ for every $x$, this product is the ordinary function type $T\to A$. An equivalence $A\simeqv B$ consists of maps $e:A\to B$ and $d:B\to A$ satisfying $d(e(a))=a$ and $e(d(b))=b$. For finite sets, this is a bijection, but explicit inverse maps also describe encoding and decoding.

\begin{definition}[Data-valued dependent-product fixed point]
A type $T$ is a data-valued dependent-product fixed point if there is a family $F:T\to\Type$ and an equivalence
\[
T\simeqv\prod_{x\in T}F(x).
\]
\end{definition}

A type is a subsingleton if any two elements are equal, and a singleton if it is both inhabited and a subsingleton.

The proposition-valued comparison uses a family $P:T\to\Prop$.

\begin{lemma}[Subsingleton section space for propositions]
For every family of propositions $P:T\to\Prop$, the product $\prod_{x\in T}P(x)$ is a subsingleton.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $s$ and $t$ be sections. At each $x$, both $s(x)$ and $t(x)$ prove the same proposition $P(x)$, so proof irrelevance gives $s(x)=t(x)$. Pointwise equality gives $s=t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Proposition-Valued Collapse]
A type $T$ satisfies
\[
\exists P:T\to\Prop,\qquad T\simeqv\prod_{x\in T}P(x)
\]
if and only if $T$ is equivalent to a singleton.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given such a family and equivalence, the lemma transfers the subsingleton property to $T$. Moreover, $T$ cannot be empty: the dependent product over an empty index type contains the unique empty section, and hence cannot be equivalent to the empty type. Thus $T$ is inhabited and a subsingleton. Conversely, if $T$ is a singleton, take every $P(x)$ to be the always-true proposition. Both sides then have one element.
\end{proof}

Hence every proposition-valued solution has decidable equality and cardinality one, all such solutions are mutually equivalent, and every predicate on such a type is extensionally constant. The fixed-point equation alone creates no nontrivial hierarchy.

\section{The Boolean dependent family}

Let
\[
\Bool=\{\false,\true\}
\]
be the Boolean type, and let $\mathbf 1=\{\unit\}$ be a singleton. Define
\[
F(\false)=\Bool,
\qquad
F(\true)=\mathbf 1.
\]
A section contains a Boolean value at $\false$ and the forced value $\unit$ at $\true$.

Define an encoder
\[
E:\Bool\longrightarrow\prod_{b\in\Bool}F(b)
\]
by
\[
E(a)(\false)=a,
\qquad
E(a)(\true)=\unit,
\]
and a decoder
\[
D:\left(\prod_{b\in\Bool}F(b)\right)\longrightarrow\Bool
\]
by $D(s)=s(\false)$.

\begin{theorem}[Boolean Data Fixed Point]
The maps $E$ and $D$ are inverse equivalences. Consequently,
\[
\Bool\simeqv\prod_{b\in\Bool}F(b).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $a\in\Bool$, $D(E(a))=E(a)(\false)=a$. Conversely, let $s$ be a section. At $\false$,
\[
E(D(s))(\false)=D(s)=s(\false).
\]
At $\true$, both $E(D(s))(\true)$ and $s(\true)$ lie in the singleton $\mathbf 1$, so both equal $\unit$. The sections are pointwise equal.
\end{proof}

The family is not a disguised constant family.

\begin{theorem}[Fiber Inequivalence]
There is no equivalence $F(\false)\simeqv F(\true)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first fiber has the two distinct Boolean elements; the second has one element. Under a hypothetical equivalence, both Boolean values would have the same image in the singleton. Injectivity would imply $\false=\true$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-Subsingleton Sections]
The section space $\prod_{b\in\Bool}F(b)$ is not a subsingleton.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The sections $E(\false)$ and $E(\true)$ differ at $\false$. Equivalently, equality of these sections would imply equality of the Booleans after applying the inverse equivalence.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Data-Valued Non-Collapse]
There exists a data-valued dependent-product fixed point that is not a subsingleton.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Take $T=\Bool$. The fixed-point equivalence is the preceding construction, and $\false$ and $\true$ are distinct.
\end{proof}

This identifies the precise failure of the proposition-valued argument: ordinary fibers need not be subsingletons, so neither must their section spaces.

\section{Finite cardinality theory}

Assume $T$ and all fibers are finite. A section is formed by independently choosing one element from each fiber.

\begin{lemma}[Finite dependent-product cardinality]
For finite $T$ and finite fibers $F(x)$,
\[
\left|\prod_{x\in T}F(x)\right|=\prod_{x\in T}|F(x)|.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induct on the finite index set. The product over no indices has one empty section, matching the empty numerical product $1$. Adding an index $x$ pairs each old section with one of the $|F(x)|$ choices at $x$, multiplying the count by $|F(x)|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Finite Cardinality Product]
If $T$ and all $F(x)$ are finite and
\[
T\simeqv\prod_{x\in T}F(x),
\]
then
\[
|T|=\prod_{x\in T}|F(x)|.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equivalent finite types have equal cardinality. Apply the preceding lemma to the section space.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Boolean Fiber Product]
For the Boolean family,
\[
\prod_{b\in\Bool}|F(b)|=2.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
The product is $|F(\false)|\,|F(\true)|=2\cdot1=2=|\Bool|$.
\end{proof}

The product law is necessary for every finite solution. Since finite sets of equal cardinality are bijective, it is also sufficient for the bare existence of some equivalence, although an explicit equivalence remains valuable because it explains where the information is stored.

For nonempty finite fibers, define information capacity as $\log_2|A|$. The product law becomes
\[
\log_2|T|=\sum_{x\in T}\log_2|F(x)|.
\]
Singleton fibers contribute no information. The Boolean construction concentrates one bit at $\false$. An empty fiber would make the section product empty, so an inhabited fixed point has no empty fiber.

If $|T|=p$ is prime, then
\[
p=\prod_{x\in T}|F(x)|.
\]
Positivity and unique factorization suggest that exactly one fiber has cardinality $p$ and all remaining fibers have cardinality $1$. The Boolean model is the first case, $p=2$.

If the family is constant, $F(x)=A$, and $n=|T|$, $a=|A|$, then the equation is
\[
n=a^n.
\]
For $n\ge2$ and $a\ge2$, one has $a^n\ge2^n>n$, so no nontrivial finite constant-fiber solution exists. Dependence avoids this exponential obstruction: the concentrated profile is $n\cdot1\cdots1=n$.

\section{Algorithms and numerical demonstrations}

For a finite ordered base $T=(x_1,\ldots,x_n)$ with listed fibers, sections can be enumerated by a Cartesian product. Begin with the empty assignment. At stage $i$, extend every partial assignment by every value of $F(x_i)$. At the end, every section appears exactly once. If $f_i=|F(x_i)|$, the number of outputs is
\[
N=\prod_{i=1}^{n}f_i.
\]
Materializing $n$-tuples takes $O(nN)$ time and storage; streaming reduces auxiliary storage but not output size. In the Boolean model,
\[
\{\false,\true\}\times\{\unit\}
=
\{(\false,\unit),(\true,\unit)\}.
\]

A cardinality checker multiplies the fiber sizes and compares the result to $|T|$. It uses $O(n)$ integer multiplications and constant list-external storage. Failure rules out an equivalence. Success proves equal finite cardinality, but does not validate proposed maps.

To verify explicit inverse maps, enumerate every state and every section, checking
\[
D(E(t))=t
\quad\text{and}\quad
E(D(s))=s.
\]
In the Boolean case the state round trips are
\[
\false\mapsto(\false,\unit)\mapsto\false,
\qquad
\true\mapsto(\true,\unit)\mapsto\true.
\]
The reverse round trips are the same rows read backward.

\section{Applications and consequences}

\paragraph{Sparse records.}
A section is a record whose field type depends on the field name. Singleton fibers are fields with forced defaults; they add structure but no variable payload. The Boolean model is the smallest sparse record: two fields, one informative and one forced.

\paragraph{Distributed state.}
The product law decomposes global state capacity into local capacities. Singleton fibers are silent coordinates. Concentrated solutions put all capacity at one location; for composite cardinalities, distributed solutions may factor capacity among several nontrivial fibers.

\paragraph{Dependent interfaces.}
A dependent interface permits a response type that varies with the query. The Boolean family presents two queries: one requests a bit, while the other permits only an acknowledgement. A complete responder is equivalent to the bit returned to the informative query.

\paragraph{Limits of structural self-reference.}
A fixed-point equation is not, by itself, an undecidability theorem. Computability-theoretic undecidability requires effective syntax, algorithms on codes, substitution, semantics or provability, and explicit soundness assumptions. The present equation is structural: it classifies representations of data rather than decision procedures.

Likewise, ordinal claims require ordinal objects and order-preserving constructions. A cardinality of fixed-point types cannot be identified with an ordinal without specifying coding, universes, a quotient by equivalence, and a relation between cardinal and ordinal notions. In the proposition-valued setting, there is already only one equivalence class.

\section{Discussion}

The Boolean model is minimal. A singleton base cannot witness non-collapse, so a two-element base is the smallest candidate. Its positive fiber cardinalities must multiply to two; up to permutation the only profile is $(2,1)$. Arithmetic therefore forces both concentration and unequal fibers.

The construction separates three levels of evidence. First, $2=2\cdot1$ shows numerical compatibility. Second, explicit encoder and decoder maps construct and explain the equivalence. Third, fiber inequivalence proves essential dependence. Larger classifications should preserve these distinctions: counting may exclude or abstractly permit a finite model, while maps reveal its computational content.

For composite $n$, multiple factor profiles may occur. A cardinality of six may be concentrated as $6\cdot1\cdots1$ or distributed as $2\cdot3\cdot1\cdots1$. Each profile gives a section set of size six, but additional criteria may distinguish canonical, symmetric, computable, or semantically meaningful equivalences.

Infinite types introduce different cardinal arithmetic. A concentrated construction also requires choosing a distinguished point and recognizing whether a queried index equals it. This motivates a general conjecture for inhabited types with decidable equality: put a copy of $T$ over one distinguished point and singleton fibers elsewhere, encode by storing the state there, and decode by evaluation. Equality transport is essential because the fiber type depends on the index.

\section{Future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Finite existence]
For every natural number $n>0$, there are a finite type $T$ with $|T|=n$ and finite fibers satisfying
\[
T\simeqv\prod_{x\in T}F(x).
\]
A proposed witness puts an $n$-element fiber at one distinguished point and singleton fibers elsewhere.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Genuine dependence]
For every $n\ge2$, such a witness can be chosen with two fibers of unequal cardinality, and therefore cannot be pointwise equivalent to a constant family.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Prime-cardinality classification]
If $|T|=p$ is prime and finite fibers satisfy the fixed-point equation, exactly one fiber has cardinality $p$ and every other fiber has cardinality $1$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Constant finite fibers are trivial]
If finite nonempty $T$ and finite $A$ satisfy $T\simeqv(T\to A)$, then either $T$ is a singleton or $A$ is a singleton. Equivalently, $n=a^n$ has no solution with $n\ge2$ and $a\ge2$.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Concentrated solutions]
Every inhabited type with decidable equality is a data-valued dependent-product fixed point, witnessed by putting all data over one distinguished point and a singleton over every other point.
\end{conjecture}

For genuine diagonal and incompleteness phenomena, a separate program should construct an enumerable code language, partial interpretation, substitution, a diagonal lemma, and a computability predicate. Universe-sensitive self-reference should similarly use codes and decoding, with positivity or guardedness controlling recursion. Any hierarchy modeled on arithmetic should first specify an effective index set and prove strictness by reductions before considering transfinite iteration.

\section{Conclusion}

Data-valued dependent-product fixed points differ fundamentally from proposition-valued ones. Proposition-valued section spaces are subsingletons, forcing the base to be a singleton. Ordinary data fibers preserve choices, and the Boolean family gives the smallest explicit non-collapsing example:
\[
F(\false)=\Bool,
\qquad
F(\true)=\mathbf1,
\qquad
\Bool\simeqv\prod_{b\in\Bool}F(b).
\]
The equivalence writes and reads the value at $\false$. Unequal fiber sizes prove genuine dependence, while two encoded sections prove non-collapse. In every finite example, the law
\[
|T|=\prod_{x\in T}|F(x)|
\]
is the first invariant and expresses conservation of finite information capacity. The identity $2=2\cdot1$ is the simplest instance and a prototype for a broader theory of concentrated and distributed dependent data.

